-- 0010: drop the GLOBAL unique on exercises.name.
--
-- Generated by removing `.unique()` from `exercises.name` in schema.ts. The
-- only statement is the drop; the per-user UNIQUE(user_id, name) added by
-- 0009 already exists and is what quick-add now targets.
--
-- WHY IT COULD NOT LAND IN 0009
-- 0009 added the per-user unique and deliberately kept the global one,
-- because program-builder.ts quick-add did:
--     .onConflictDoNothing({ target: exercises.name })
-- A compound target raises at runtime unless the global unique is present.
-- This is the commit that removes that call, so it is the first point at which
-- no code needs the global constraint — and the first point the drop is safe.
--
-- REVERSIBLE. The reverse is:
--     ALTER TABLE "exercises" ADD CONSTRAINT "exercises_name_unique" UNIQUE("name");
-- The per-user unique is untouched either way, so a re-add of the global one
-- can fail only if two users already share an exercise name by that point.
--
-- CONSTRAINT NAME: 0001 declared `name` as inline UNIQUE via
-- pgTable('exercises').unique(), which drizzle-kit emitted under this name;
-- the 0009 audit confirmed the live constraint uses the same name, so the
-- generated drop matches what is actually in the database.
--
-- This is NOT the irreversible NOT NULL step. That is 0011_ownership_not_null,
-- the last commit of T3, and the coupling rule is unchanged: no T3 code
-- deploys to a populated database before 0011.
ALTER TABLE "exercises" DROP CONSTRAINT "exercises_name_unique";
